## What was wrong
RTF import treated the document-level page words (`\paperw`, `\margl`, `\landscape`) and the section-level ones (`\pgwsxn`, `\marglsxn`, `\lndscpsxn`) as one setting, and `\sect` only ended a paragraph. So a multi-section RTF, such as the four-section file attached to #1378, became one section. The last orientation won, columns and page-number restarts were lost, and so were the later sections' own headers.

## What changed
**`crates/formats/src/rtf.rs`** (import; RTF export still writes one section):
- Document defaults and the current section's settings are now separate. `\sectd` resets the section, as the RTF 1.9.1 spec says, and section settings carry into the next section until the next `\sectd`. A section's settings cover:
  - page size, orientation and margins;
  - header and footer distance;
  - break type (`\sbknone`/`col`/`page`/`even`/`odd`);
  - columns (`\cols`, `\colsx`, `\linebetcol`);
  - page numbering (`\pgnrestart`/`\pgncont`, `\pgnstarts`, and the decimal, Roman and letter formats);
  - `\titlepg`.
- `\sect` ends a section: its settings and the headers and footers read since the last break go on the section-break paragraph. A section without its own header or footer links to the previous one, as in Word and DOCX.
- A section's own orientation wins over a document-level `\landscape`.
- Section words inside header and footer groups are ignored. Word writes a `\sectd` inside footer field results, and the #1378 file does this.
- A trailing `\sect` adds no empty section. `\sect` inside a table cell only ends the paragraph, as before.
- Hostile input is bounded:
  - at most 10,000 sections;
  - header and footer parts bounded per section;
  - columns clamped to 1–45, column spacing to 0–1584 pt, and the start page to 0–32767.

