Fixes #3209.

## Root cause

Confirmed on macOS (the issue) and independently on Linux (a comment on the issue). Both report the same shape: opening a `.docx` by double-clicking it (OS file association) lands on the "Recover unsaved documents" backstage instead of the document — but the document is actually loaded correctly underneath; clicking the back button reveals it immediately, no recovery needed. Opening the same file via File › Open from inside a running WordCraft never triggers it.

The one-shot check in `crates/ui-egui/src/lib.rs` (`WordApp::logic`) that pops the Recover screen only looks at whether there happen to be any leftover recovery copies and whether the session is `dirty`:

```rust
if !self.recovery_seen && self.session.recovery_ready() {
    self.recovery_seen = true;
    if !self.session.recovery_documents().is_empty() && !self.session.dirty {
        self.ui.backstage = true;
        self.ui.backstage_page = "recover".into();
    }
}
```

When a file is handed to the app at launch (`apps/wordcraft/src/main.rs` calls `file.open` synchronously before the event loop's first tick, for both a CLI argument and an OS double-click), `session.path` is already `Some(...)` and `session.dirty` is `false` (a freshly opened, unmodified document) from the very first frame. If there's *any* unrelated leftover recovery copy sitting in the profile — from a previous crash, a previous test session, anything — this condition is satisfied and the Recover backstage is shown over the document the user explicitly asked to open.

`recovery_seen` only fires once per app lifetime, which is why File › Open (reached well after startup) never shows this: by then the one-shot check has already resolved.

## Fix

Add `self.session.path.is_none()` to the condition: only auto-prompt for recovery on a genuinely blank launch, not when a specific file was already handed to the session. Recovery documents aren't lost — `file.recover` is still reachable manually (the "Recover Unsaved…

