## Root cause

Autosaving and discarding a recovery copy both go through a background worker thread (`crates/engine/src/recovery/desktop.rs`'s `Recovery`), the same architecture as the print spooler. `Recovery::tick` already does the right thing conceptually — once the document becomes clean (`!s.dirty`, what a successful Save does), it queues discarding this session's own now-redundant recovery copy:

```rust
if !s.dirty || s.password.is_some() {
    if let Some(id) = self.id.clone() {
        self.queue(Job::Discard(id))?;
    }
    return Ok(());
}
```

But `queue` just sends the job to the worker thread and returns — it doesn't wait for it to actually finish. If the app closes right after Save (the very next frame, before any further `recovery_tick()` notices the clean document and queues the discard at all, or with the discard queued but the worker hasn't gotten to it yet), the process can exit before the stale record is actually removed from disk. The next launch then finds it and wrongly offers to recover a document that was in fact saved and closed cleanly.

This repo already solved the identical problem for print jobs: `apps/wordcraft/src/print.rs` keeps worker `JoinHandle`s around and `finish_pending_jobs()` joins them before exit, called from both `on_exit` and the macOS hard-quit path (`schedule_macos_terminate`) specifically because — per that code's own comment — "dropping a JoinHandle would let process exit kill PowerShell/CUPS before it hands the job to the spooler." Recovery had no equivalent.

## Fix

Added `Recovery::finish` (mirroring the existing `purge` method's "queue then block on the response channel" pattern already used for the explicit protect-with-password path) and a `Session::finish_recovery` wrapper, and call it from the same two places `print::finish_pending_jobs()` is already called from.

