Laying out a line beside a floating object measured the word that starts on it by walking to the end of the paragraph, so a document that carries one very long token took minutes to lay out.

### Cause
`crates/layout/src/para.rs` measures the word that starts a line, to tell whether it fits the full line beside a floating object:

```rust
let word: f32 = (c0..n)
    .filter_map(|k| pl.clusters.get(k).map(|c| (k, c)))
    .scan(false, |done, (k, c)| {
        let end = c.break_after && pl.hyph_after.binary_search(&(k as u32)).is_err();
        (!std::mem::replace(done, end)).then_some(c)
    })
    .filter(|c| c.kind != ClKind::Space)
    .map(|c| c.adv)
    .sum();
let full = base_right - row_lo;
skip = narrowed && skipped < 50 && word <= full + 0.01;
```

The `scan` ends at the first cluster it doesn't yield, so the word is the clusters up to the first break opportunity — but the walk costs the rest of the paragraph however short the word is. A word with no break opportunity in it gives a line per word's worth of characters, and each of those lines walks the paragraph again: the layout is quadratic in the word's length.

The same text as ordinary prose lays out in 0.39 s for a megabyte, 76 times faster, because a space ends the word and the scan stops there.

### Change
- The measurement stops once the word is clearly wider than the room there is: the answer is known by then, and the advances are widths, so the rest of the word could only make it wider. A font can pull a glyph back with a negative advance, though, so the rest of the word is measured only as far as it could take the width back. That bound stops at the break that ends the word, so the words after a long word cannot hold the measurement up.
- The answer and the layout are the same: one line's worth of clusters per line instead of a paragraph's.
