Opening a document looked for each relationship's id in the main document's part, once per relationship, so a document with many relationships spent minutes of CPU on a file that is small on disk.

### Cause
`crates/docx/src/read/embed.rs` keeps the parts of a package that WordCraft neither models nor writes itself, so that saving carries them over unchanged. To skip a relationship the main document's markup already refers to, it looked for that relationship's id in the main document's part:

```rust
let main = pkg.get(&self.main).unwrap_or_default();
let referenced = |id: &str| {
    let needle = format!("\"{id}\"");
    main.windows(needle.len()).any(|w| w == needle.as_bytes())
};
```

That is a full pass over the part, once per relationship. With the reader's limits — `MAX_PART` = 256 MiB for one part and `MAX_EMBEDDED_RELS` = 1024 for the relationships of one part — the worst case is about 256 GiB of byte comparisons for one document.

### Change
- The ids the main document refers to are the ones it names in a relationship attribute (`r:id`, VML's `o:relid`) — the same rule the embedded objects use. The reader has already parsed the main document, so the ids come from that tree: one pass over the markup it has, no search over the part, and no second parse. The set of ids is bounded by the relationships the reader looks at, so a file with a large relationship part does not make the open allocate for ids no lookup can reach.
- An id that only appears as text is not a reference. The text names no part, and treating it as one lost the part on save: the relationship was skipped as "already referred to", so its target was carried neither by the element that names it nor as an unmodelled part. A part whose id a paragraph only mentions is now carried over instead of dropped.
