A command that changed nothing was still an edit: it added an undo step, marked the document edited, forced a relayout and threw the redo stack away.

### Cause
`Session::run` pushes the undo step before the command runs and marks the document edited after it succeeds. Nothing compares the document before and after:

```rust
if spec.mutates {
    let label = if typing { "Typing" } else { spec.label };
    if !join || self.history.is_empty() {
        self.checkpoint(label);   // also clears the redo stack
    }
}
...
Ok(_) => {
    if spec.mutates {
        ...
        self.touch();             // revision bumped (layout cache dropped), document marked edited
```

So `text.insert {"text": ""}`, Backspace at the start of the document, Enter in a one-line content control — anything that changes nothing — added a step. The history is bounded by step count alone (`MAX_UNDO = 500`), so five hundred of them filled the whole stack and pushed the user's own edits out of it: the evicted steps are gone for good, and the text typed before them could never be undone again.

### Change
- `crates/doc/src/lib.rs`: a new `Document::same_content` says whether a document holds the same content as another. The blocks are compared by address first — the document is copy-on-write, so a block that changed was copied — and a block that was copied but holds the same content is compared by value; everything else (styles, lists, settings, comments, revisions, metadata, parts, pictures) is compared by value. Every field of `Document` is listed, so a new one does not build until someone decides whether a change to it counts, the same rule the session's edit snapshot follows.
