Tables were exported to tagged PDF as their cells' paragraphs one after another. A screen reader could not announce "table, 4 rows, 3 columns", move by row or column, or read a cell with its column header.

A table is now tagged following the structure Word's own PDF export uses:

- the table is a `Table`; its leading header rows (Table Properties › Row › Repeat as header row) are in `THead` with `TH` cells, `Scope` `Column`; the other rows are in `TBody` with `TD` cells;
- every cell of the table is there, empty ones included, so the grid read out matches the page;
- a merged cell is one cell with `ColSpan` (horizontal merge) or `RowSpan` (vertical merge); the rows it covers have no cell of their own there;
- a cell's content stays in its cell: paragraphs, lists (#4257), inline pictures as `Figure`, nested tables as a `Table` inside the cell;
- a row split across pages, or a cell whose text runs onto the next page, is still one row and one cell: content is gathered by its table, row and cell, not by where it falls on the page;
- a header row repeated at the top of a later page is drawn as before but tagged as an artifact, so it is read once, where the table starts.

Before: `Document(P P P P P P P P P P P)`. After, for the first test document: `Document(P Table(THead(TR(TH(P) TH(P) TH(P))) TBody(TR(TD(P) TD(P)) TR(TD(P) TD(P) TD(P)) TR(TD(P) TD))) P)`.

To place content, each structure entry now remembers its story and paragraph path; the paths of cell paragraphs already say which table, row and cell they are in. Pictures inline in a cell paragraph, and floating pictures anchored in a cell, go in that cell too.

